Z||000007||Gold-86SE||03-14-2001||05:48 PM||||<BR>I may have missed it, but have you checked your codes?<P>Also, have you replaced the fuel filter?<P>Is your throttle body caked with carbon deposits?<P>Add some fuel injector cleaner to your fuel, see if that helps.||||reg||1
Z||000008||JC Webb||03-14-2001||06:07 PM||||I agree that it sounds like it is fuel related.  As mentioned above first check the fuel filter.  The backfiring you are hearing through the exhaust is a product of not having a cat.  When I bought my 86 SE it did not have a cat and backfired on the way down.  But in my opinion it sounded good!||||reg||1
Z||000009||stsmithgt||03-14-2001||06:29 PM||||Mine did the same thing.  The ol' drive for a while and strand you down at the store sort of game.  I noticed if you can get it moving, you can floor it and go through the gears.  The higher the speed limit the better.  When you level off your speed, it's back to sputter, poof, cough, slow down and do it again.  Reminded me of an old, one barrel Rochester carburater I used to have on a Chevy...  If you don't have codes showing and your timing is correct, I haven't heard anyone tell you it might be your fuel pump failing.  The tank needs to be dropped to get to it so an empty tank would be ideal.<BR>  I was told it could be couple of different sensors.  One or more of them could be bad, AND/OR/ALSO it was my fuel pump.  I had the sensors replaced and it didn't cure the problem, but it did make a code or two go away. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Then the pump was replaced.  Found out I had some aftermarket P.O.S. in there from a previous fix.(first owner).  It's worked great ever since. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ¢¢||||reg||4
Z||000010||WKDFIRO||03-15-2001||01:33 AM||||If it was the fuel pump you would not be able to stand on it and go through the gears, the pump would not be able to keep up with demand when it goes south...but it would be a good idea to put a gauge on it to find out...<P>When you did the tune up did you also replace the pickup coil and module? No tune up would be complete without those!<P>Quite the mystery you have there...hope we can help!||||reg||5
Z||000011||Steven||03-15-2001||10:01 AM||||Sounds like something that has happened to my car. Bad news for me was that my timing chain had jumped. I hope that isn't what happened to you.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Oreif||03-15-2001||10:18 AM||||Here are some causes I have seen that cause your particular problem.<BR>Bad Catalytic converter.<BR>Bad ignition module in distributor. <BR>Weak ingnition coil.<BR>(The above get heat sensitive when they get old)<BR>Fault Fuel pressure regulator.<BR>Faulty Fuel pump.<BR>Incorrect timing.<P>My guess would be the ignition module. To prove it's the module that is causing the problems, You could do this little experiment. Drive the car until it starts to mess up. Then turn car off and remove the lighter cover on the console and short out the top left 2 pins (when facing connector)<BR>Then start the car and see how it idles. This puts the ECM in open loop and causes your ignition timing to remain constant. If it still sputters and pops the ignition module is bad (still could be the ignition coil too). If it runs fine then some other sensor is bad telling the ECM to screw with the timing.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||wjluciano||03-15-2001||08:37 PM||||I agree with gold 86 check the fuel filter also check for moister in the distributer cap<BR>||||reg||14
